  "full_title": "The Nativity with the Annunciation to the Shepherds and the Adoration of the Shepherds: Upper Tier Panel",
  "short_title": "The Nativity with the Annunciation to the Shepherds",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "Gothic",
  "material": "egg tempera on wood",
  "dimensions": "95.5 × 49.4 cm",
  "production_date": "1370-1",
  "brief_description": "This panel was the first in a series showing scenes from Christ's life, the Resurrection and his ascension to heaven and was part of a four-tiered altarpiece made for the Florentine church of San Pier Maggiore. These panels sat above the main tier of images showing the heavenly coronation of the Virgin.\n\nThe scene shows the Christ Child in a manger, borrowed from the ox and donkey who sit nearby. The Virgin Mary sits on the ground worshipping the Child. Two shepherds, tucked around the corner of a craggy rock, look on and pray. In the hillside above, we see the moment when they were told about Christ's birth: an angel has appeared to them, lighting up the night sky with a golden glow that colours the nearby trees. This is one of the earliest night scenes in a panel painting.",
